  {
   "id": "7UbW-DaWAgAJ",
   "from": "Michael Betancourt",
   "author": "a5e83218c834e013",
   "date": "2016-07-15T12:53:52Z",
   "subject": "Re: [stan-users] Divergent transitions in meta-regression model",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\">Just a quick note —divergences simply indicate that Stan cannot fit the model, it does<div>not necessarily imply that the model is incompatible with the data (although there’s often</div><div>a strong correlation, which is the basis of the “Folk Theorem”).  But it’s important to take</div><div>divergences seriously, because if you’re not getting an accurate fit then you can’t really</div><div>judge the compatibility of your model!</div><div><br></div><div>Upon a very brief look your model looks appropriate — but perhaps there might be too</div><div>many random effects and what you are seeing is a consequently of extreme linear</div><div>correlations (as opposed to the nonlinear correlations that we typical worry about in</div><div>these hierarchical models).</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\"><div><div><br><div><div>On Jul 15, 2016, at 8:24 AM, Lauren Vollmer &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">vollmer...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:</div><br><blockquote type=\"cite\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"font-family:Helvetica;font-size:11px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:Arial,sans-serif\">I&#39;m curious about how others approach this question from a reproducibility and modeling robustness standpoint; if the model ever produces divergent transitions, should I assume it&#39;s mis-specified and revise accordingly?<u></u><u></u></span></p><br></blockquote></div><br></div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "yrKvPaWjAgAJ",
   "from": "Lauren Vollmer",
   "author": "7a587da939a18ce9",
   "date": "2016-07-15T17:00:13Z",
   "subject": "Re: [stan-users] Divergent transitions in meta-regression model",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Thank you!  Your comment on the number of random effects reminded my collaborator of a solution for scenarios where we wish to model several categorical variables, each with comparatively few levels, as in this case: to borrow strength across the variance components.  We did so, following the recommendation in Gelman and Hill (p. 500), which eliminated the divergent transitions.  The final code is attached and below.<div><br></div><div>Gratefully yours, </div><div>Lauren</div><div><br></div><div>----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</div><div><br></div><div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div>data{</div><div>   int&lt;lower=1&gt; N;    </div><div>   int&lt;lower=1&gt; nAs;</div><div>   int&lt;lower=1&gt; nBs;</div><div>   int&lt;lower=1&gt; nCs;</div><div>   int&lt;lower=1&gt; nDs;</div><div><br></div><div>   int&lt;lower=1, upper=nAs&gt; A[N];</div><div>   int&lt;lower=1, upper=nBs&gt; B[N];</div><div>   int&lt;lower=1, upper=nCs&gt; C[N]; </div><div>   int&lt;lower=1, upper=nDs&gt; D[N];</div><div>   vector[N] y;</div><div>   vector[N] y_se;</div><div><br></div><div>}</div><div><br></div><div>parameters{</div><div><br></div><div>   real alpha;</div><div>   vector[nAs] a_raw;              </div><div>   vector[nBs] b_raw;              </div><div>   vector[nCs] c_raw;              </div><div>   vector[nDs] d_raw;              </div><div><br></div><div>   real&lt;lower=0&gt; sigma_a;</div><div>   real&lt;lower=0&gt; sigma_b;</div><div>   real&lt;lower=0&gt; sigma_c;</div><div>   real&lt;lower=0&gt; sigma_d;</div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div>   real&lt;lower=0&gt; sigma_epsilon;</div><div>   <font color=\"#9900ff\">real&lt;lower = 0, upper = 10&gt; phi;</font></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div>}</div><div><br></div><div>transformed parameters{</div><div><br></div><div>   vector[nAs] a;</div><div>   vector[nBs] b;</div><div>   vector[nCs] c;</div><div>   vector[nDs] d;</div><div><br></div><div>   a &lt;- a_raw*sigma_a;</div><div>   b &lt;- b_raw*sigma_b;</div><div>   c &lt;- c_raw*sigma_c;</div><div>   d &lt;- d_raw*sigma_d;</div><div>}</div><div><br></div><div>model{</div><div>  real yHat[N];</div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div>  real ySd[N];</div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><br></div><div>  for(i in 1:N){</div><div>     yHat[i] &lt;- alpha + a[A[i]] + b[B[i]] + c[C[i]] + d[D[i]];</div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div>     ySd[i] &lt;- sqrt(pow(y_se[i], 2) + pow(sigma_epsilon, 2));</div><div>    }</div><div><br></div><div>  y ~ normal(yHat, ySd);</div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><br></div><div>  alpha ~ normal(0, 10);</div><div>  a_raw ~ normal(0, 1);  </div><div>  b_raw ~ normal(0, 1);</div><div>  c_raw ~ normal(0, 1);</div><div>  d_raw ~ normal(0, 1);</div><div><br></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div>  sigma_a ~ cauchy(0, <font color=\"#9900ff\">phi</font>);</div><div><div>  sigma_b ~ cauchy(0, <font color=\"#9900ff\">phi</font>);</div><div>  sigma_c ~ cauchy(0, <font color=\"#9900ff\">phi</font>);</div><div>  sigma_d ~ cauchy(0, <font color=\"#9900ff\">phi</font>);</div><div>  sigma_epsilon ~ cauchy(0, <font color=\"#9900ff\">phi</font>);</div><div>}</div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br>On Friday, July 15, 2016 at 8:53:52 AM UTC-4, Michael Betancourt wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\">Just a quick note —divergences simply indicate that Stan cannot fit the model, it does<div>not necessarily imply that the model is incompatible with the data (although there’s often</div><div>a strong correlation, which is the basis of the “Folk Theorem”).  But it’s important to take</div><div>divergences seriously, because if you’re not getting an accurate fit then you can’t really</div><div>judge the compatibility of your model!</div><div><br></div><div>Upon a very brief look your model looks appropriate — but perhaps there might be too</div><div>many random effects and what you are seeing is a consequently of extreme linear</div><div>correlations (as opposed to the nonlinear correlations that we typical worry about in</div><div>these hierarchical models).</div><div><div><br><div><div>On Jul 15, 2016, at 8:24 AM, Lauren Vollmer &lt;<a rel=\"nofollow\">vollmer...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:</div><br><blockquote type=\"cite\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"font-family:Helvetica;font-size:11px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:Arial,sans-serif\">I&#39;m curious about how others approach this question from a reproducibility and modeling robustness standpoint; if the model ever produces divergent transitions, should I assume it&#39;s mis-specified and revise accordingly?</span></p><br></blockquote></div><br></div></div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  }
 ]
